WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.820
A dangling pointer is a pointer that still holds an address after the referenced object is no longer valid.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:16.360
The pointer might live in a local variable, structure field, array slot, cache entry, callback context, global state, or object graph.

3
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:22.300
The defect appears when later code treats that stale pointer as though the object still exists.

4
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.340
Dangling pointers often remain after freeing through another alias.

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.760
Aliasing means more than one pointer can refer to the same allocation.

6
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:36.620
If one alias releases the allocation, every other alias that still points to that storage becomes stale.

7
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.720
Setting the releasing variable to null does not update the other aliases.

8
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:51.320
Pointers stored in structures, arrays, caches, callback contexts, and global state are especially important because they can outlive the code path that created them.

9
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.100
A temporary-looking pointer may become long-lived once it is stored.

10
00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.360
Reviewers should ask not only where a pointer is created, but where it is retained.

11
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:07.800
Returning or storing references without understanding lifetime creates stale-reference risk.

12
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.020
A function might return a borrowed pointer into an object that the caller later destroys.

13
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.120
A callback might keep a pointer to state that belongs to a request object.

14
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.940
A cleanup routine might run while a queued operation still refers to the same memory.

15
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.900
Setting one pointer to null after release can help prevent accidental reuse through that one variable.

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.800
It is not a complete alias-management strategy.

17
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:38.180
Other variables, structure fields, containers, callbacks, or cached pointers may still hold the old address.

18
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.480
The deeper practice is knowing who else can reach the allocation before releasing it.

19
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.520
Developers should identify all reachable references before releasing shared or referenced memory.

20
00:01:49.620 --> 00:02:00.820
If several components can see an object, the design needs a clear owner, a borrowing rule, a release point, and a way to prevent later users from treating stale references as valid.
